This code is only a suggestion. You may want to improve it according to your own requirements. Let me explain how it works:

1. Scan through each comment under each method in your C# code to find the comments starting with "@@@". 

2. Extract the statement from each such comment and store it in a list. If the statement needs to access a variable before the function executes (as indicated by the `OLD` syntax), search for occurrences of "OLD(VARIABLE_NAME)" in the assert statement, replacing each occurrence with the corresponding variable's value. If the assert statement refers to the function's return value (denoted by the "RET" string), replace that occurrence with the return value. 

3. Finally, print each assert statement in your desired format, e.g. "Debug.Assert(SomeAssertion);". 

The logic of how to find variables and get their values will depend on your specific C# environment and needs. For example, your solution might involve a C# AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) to read variables and a method to interpret your C# bytecode. This can be far more complex and may need additional logic to handle nested blocks, control flow structures, etc.--------------------The task you have set is to extract comments starting with "@@@" from the C# code and transform them into Assert statements. We will use the .NET Regex class to match the comments starting with "@@@" and parse their content into an Assert statement. The regular expression used will look like this: 

```
@\{\s*\/\/\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\}|\/\/\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\n|\/\*\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\*\/
```

Note that \/\/\s* allows for any whitespace characters at the beginning of the comment, \@\@\@ for "@@@" at the beginning of the line and (.*) matches everything till the end of the line, if there is any. If there is multiple lines comments, it can end with multiple asterisks.

Here is a simple way to do this in C#:

```C#
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

namespace Program
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string code = @"
```cs
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < y; i++){
        x = x + 1;
    }
    // @@@ Value of x increases by y in the function
    return x;
}
```";

            Regex regex = new Regex(@"@\{\s*\/\/\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\}|\/\/\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\n|\/\*\s*\@\@\@\s*(.*)\*\/", RegexOptions.Multiline);

            MatchCollection matches = regex.Matches(code);

            foreach (Match match in matches)
            {
                string comment = match.Value.Trim();
                comment = comment.Replace("// ", string.Empty);
                comment = comment.Replace("/* ", string.Empty);
                comment = comment.Replace(" */", string.Empty);

                string assertStatement = $"\tDebug.Assert({comment});\n";

                Console.WriteLine(assertStatement);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Please note this example is only taking the comments that are present inside code blocks and are part of the multiline comments (either ``` */ */ ``` or ``` // ``` ). This approach doesn't consider comments that are directly included in the code or are multiline comments outside code blocks.

